deteriorated year-on-year, while the loss ratio was broadly flat at 72.9% versus 72.8%.","risk":"Underwriting result remains loss-making at the combined-ratio level and widened during the year"},{"note":"\"IRDAI requires insurance companies to maintain a minimum solvency of 1.5 times which is calculated in a manner as specified in the IRDAI (Actuarial, Finance and Investment Functions of Insurers) Regulations, 2024\"; the Company stood at 2.42 times as at March 31, 2026 versus 2.24 times a year earlier, so premium growth is permanently tied to maintaining regulatory capital.","risk":"Regulatory solvency floor constrains growth and capital flexibility"},{"note":"The Scheme is \"conditional upon receipt of observation/no-objection letters from the Stock Exchanges\", approval by the requisite majority of each class of creditors and members, votes in favour exceeding those against, \"approvals from the Competition Commission of India and the IRDAI under the Insurance Act, 1938\" and sanction by the NCLT — a multi-regulator chain with no stated completion date, and the Appointed Date is itself the Effective Date.","risk":"Execution and approval risk on the proposed amalgamation of the holding company"},{"note":"\"the trademarks and brand names 'DIGIT' and 'GO DIGIT', together with other related trademarks central to the Company's business and brand identity, were assigned by Go Digit Infoworks Services Private Limited ('the holding Company') to Go Digit Solutions Private Limited, a Promoter Group entity\"; the earlier licence was terminated and a new licence executed, so the Company's core brand right is contractual and rests with a promoter-group party.","risk":"Brand is owned outside the Company and used under licence"},{"note":"\"The net retention ratio and the net earning ratio ... were 73.7% and 101.1% respectively as against 80.0% and 97.8% respectively for the previous financial year\" — a lower retention ratio means a larger share of risk is ceded, making outcomes more dependent on reinsurer capacity, pricing and recoveries.","risk":"Reduced net retention increases dependence on reinsurance arrangements"},{"note":"\"The industry's Gross Direct Premium ('GDP') grew from ₹ 3,07,66,069 Lakhs in FY2024-25 to ₹ 3,36,12,268 Lakhs in FY2025-26, marking a growth of 9.3%\" — the Company writes general insurance in India only, with market share of 3.4% versus 3.3%, so its top line moves with Indian non-life industry growth and share shifts.","risk":"Growth is tied to a single industry and geography whose own growth is cyclical"}],"rpt":{},"rpt_date":"","s":"GODIGIT","sector":"Financial Services","sk":{"as_of":"2026-06-15","cagrs":[{"cagr":"~10.3%","period":"FY2020-FY2025","segment":"Retail insurance market GDPI (health + life new business + motor combined)"},{"cagr":"~18%","period":"FY2020-FY2025","segment":"Health insurance GDPI"},{"cagr":"14-16%","period":"FY2025-FY2030","segment":"Health insurance GDPI (projected)"},{"cagr":"14-17%","period":"FY2025-FY2030","segment":"B30+ motor GDPI"},{"cagr":"17-19%","period":"FY2025-FY2030","segment":"B30+ health GDPI"},{"cagr":"10-11%","period":"FY2025-FY2030","segment":"B30+ life new business GDPI"}],"drivers":"Rising incomes and protection-linked spend (Swiss Re S-curve)|Digital adoption (smartphones, UPI ~65% CAGR)|IRDAI 'Insurance for All by 2047' and regulatory enablement of PoSP/digital onboarding|Low insurance penetration vs peers; B30+ under-penetration","industry":"Insurance","market_size":[{"metric":"India total GDPI (all premiums)","value":"~Rs 11.9 trillion (US$140 billion)","year":"FY2025"},{"metric":"India GDP","value":"~Rs 332 trillion (US$3.91 trillion)","year":"FY2025"},{"metric":"India GDP (projected)","value":"Rs 563 trillion (US$6.6 trillion), 3rd-largest economy","year":"FY2031"}],"overview":"India's insurance distribution market is transforming from fragmented offline agent/broker/bancassurance channels toward tech-enabled 'phygital' brokers using the PoSP model. Rising i